{"id":"h105","run":2,"role":"test_wrong","category":"science-chemistry","question":"Calculate theCoulombicbarrier in the bombardment of ^209Bi Calculate theCoulombicbarrier in the bombardment of ^209Bi with alpha particles.\nIs the correct answer \"21.2MeV\"?","truth":"yes","model_answer":"no","correct":false,"source":"TIGER-Lab/MMLU-Pro, test split","claims":[{"text":"The correct answer to the question is not 21.2 MeV.","confidence":0.9,"lean":-1,"theta":1.4137,"phi":-3.1416},{"text":"The standard touching-sphere calculation gives a Coulomb barrier of about 26.5 MeV.","confidence":0.92,"lean":-1,"theta":1.4451,"phi":-3.1416},{"text":"The standard radius parameter is r0 = 1.2 fm, with R = r0 A^(1/3).","confidence":0.9,"lean":-0.6,"theta":1.4137,"phi":-3.1416},{"text":"The sum of the alpha and Bi-209 radii is about 9.02 fm.","confidence":0.9,"lean":-0.5,"theta":1.4137,"phi":-3.1416},{"text":"The 21.2 MeV value is reproduced only if r0 is about 1.5 fm.","confidence":0.8,"lean":-0.5,"theta":1.2566,"phi":-3.1416},{"text":"Some sources use a larger effective radius for the nuclear surface, so 21.2 MeV could be a valid textbook or empirical figure.","confidence":0.35,"lean":0.5,"theta":0.5498,"phi":0.0},{"text":"21.2 MeV does not follow from the standard formula and needs a nonstandard radius parameter.","confidence":0.85,"lean":-0.8,"theta":1.3352,"phi":-3.1416},{"text":"Under the usual textbook convention, 21.2 MeV is not the correct answer.","confidence":0.85,"lean":-0.9,"theta":1.3352,"phi":-3.1416}],"relations":[{"i":0,"j":1,"J":0.8,"relation":"supports"},{"i":0,"j":2,"J":0.4,"relation":"supports"},{"i":0,"j":3,"J":0.3,"relation":"supports"},{"i":0,"j":4,"J":0.5,"relation":"supports"},{"i":0,"j":5,"J":-0.7,"relation":"contradicts"},{"i":0,"j":6,"J":0.6,"relation":"supports"},{"i":0,"j":7,"J":0.95,"relation":"supports"},{"i":1,"j":2,"J":0.7,"relation":"supports"},{"i":1,"j":3,"J":0.8,"relation":"supports"},{"i":1,"j":6,"J":0.6,"relation":"supports"},{"i":1,"j":7,"J":0